---
<DocGuide chapter="system" title="Lucent and Omarchy ownership map" heading="Know who owns" accent="every desktop layer." description="A source-based map of native Lucent UI, retained Omarchy behavior, system services and unfinished replacements." sections={sections} audience="Desktop integration guide">
  <section id="startup"><span class="eyebrow">01 / WHAT ACTUALLY STARTS</span><h2>The stock shell still runs.</h2>
    <p>The current Lucent integration starts a user service from a marked Hyprland autostart block. Its Python supervisor launches the Rust desktop, waits for rendered bar/dock/widget surfaces, hands notification ownership to Lucent, then hides the stock bar. It keeps the original visibility and notification settings for recovery.</p>
    <p>This is a partial shell replacement. Omarchy's background, polkit, idle integration and specialized IPC paths remain available. A stock-shell restart is sometimes needed to release its notification bus name, and the handoff refuses to interrupt an active secure stock lock. On failure or service stop, the supervisor restores the previous stock state.</p>
    <CodeBlock code={`Omarchy Hyprland session
  ├─ stock shell / background / permission and session services
  └─ user autostart → lucent.service
       → lucent-session.py supervisor
       → lucent-desktop
       → render surfaces → notification handoff → hide stock bar

Stop / crash → supervisor and ExecStopPost restore stock state`} label="Current startup ownership; not a fully removed stock shell" />
    <p>Read the implementation: <a href={sourceUrl('scripts/lucent-session.py')}>handoff supervisor</a> and <a href={sourceUrl('scripts/lucent-setup.py')}>user-level installer</a>. Removing the remaining stock process requires replacing its responsibilities first; uninstalling it now would break existing behavior.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="ownership"><span class="eyebrow">02 / CURRENT RESPONSIBILITIES</span><h2>Rendering and behavior<br />can have different owners.</h2>
    <div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Capability</th><th>Owner</th><th>Current behavior</th><th>Configuration / implementation</th></tr></thead><tbody>{ownership.map(([feature, owner, behavior, source]) => <tr><td>{feature}</td><td>{owner}</td><td>{behavior}</td><td>{source}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
    <p>This maps the integrations implemented in this repository. User-installed applications and custom menu extensions can add commands outside this list. Hardware-specific Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, battery, brightness and physical display behavior require hardware tests; a VM does not prove them.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="rendering"><span class="eyebrow">03 / WHO DRAWS WHAT</span><h2>Lucent paints its surfaces.<br />Hyprland composes the screen.</h2>
    <p>The reusable runtime creates Wayland layer surfaces. The bar requests a top strip. The dock/launcher draws inside a transparent full-output surface; widgets use a layer below ordinary windows. The desktop's geometry determines the visible panels and input regions inside those surfaces. Widgets are not tiled application windows.</p>
    <p>CPU code samples animations and computes layout. Our renderer translates paint instructions into GPU resources and commands through wgpu's Vulkan backend. Hyprland combines the rendered buffers with other applications and the stock background. Chrome and the terminal draw their own contents; Hyprland supplies their compositor decorations.</p>
    <p>Secure lock surfaces use the session-lock protocol, not the ordinary launcher overlay. The greeter runs in a separate login environment. These clients share framework and design code while retaining different security and lifecycle responsibilities.</p>
    <p>See <a href={withBase('/docs/library/')}>the library guide</a> for event-loop and renderer details, or <a href={withBase('/docs/desktop/')}>desktop configuration</a> for practical settings.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="menus"><span class="eyebrow">04 / KEEP EXISTING ACTIONS</span><h2>Native picker.<br />Installed definitions.</h2>
    <p>The menu adapter reads <code>/usr/share/omarchy/default/omarchy/omarchy-menu.jsonc</code> (or the configured Omarchy root), then merges <code>~/.config/omarchy/extensions/omarchy-menu.jsonc</code>. User actions take precedence. Select/input wrappers return the exact original selected value after releasing keyboard focus.</p>
    <p>Known routes explicitly map background selection to Lucent's carousel and app selection to Lucent's launcher. Omarchy theme, font and power-profile providers still execute their original tools. Other actions retain their installed behavior, including direct stock-shell IPC. The adapter does not infer ownership by guessing command names.</p>
    <p>Trusted menu configuration may contain shell expressions. Application desktop entries, labels, search input and dynamic provider values are handled separately; arbitrary user-entered text is not turned into a shell command. Read <a href={sourceUrl('docs/native-menu.md')}>the full menu contract</a>.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="verify"><span class="eyebrow">05 / CHECK THE CURRENT SESSION</span><h2>Inspect before changing integration.</h2>
    <CodeBlock code="systemctl --user status lucent.service\nlucent-cli inspect\nhyprctl -j binds\nhyprctl -j layers\npgrep -a -f 'lucent|quickshell|omarchy-shell'" label="Read-only commands inside the guest; process arguments can contain local paths" />
    <p>The source map is the contract for the prepared installation. Omarchy updates can change menu definitions and startup services. Review the installed definitions and user overrides after updates, then test both the native path and rollback. Keep local dumps and screenshots containing personal information out of Git.</p>
  </section>
</DocGuide>
